<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stephen, King of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1092–<a href="/years/1154">1154</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Stephen of Blois, King Stephen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stephen was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from 1135 to 1154, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> through his mother Adela and a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-count-of-blois">Stephen-Henry</a>, Count of Blois. He became count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> by marrying its heiress Matilda and seized the English throne on the death of Henry I, against the claim of Empress Matilda. Their struggle, known as the Anarchy, ended when Stephen accepted her son Henry as his heir.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stephen-king-of-england.jpg" alt="Stephen, King of England" width="210" height="522" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry of Huntingdon (Historia Anglorum) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stepan_kral.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stephen was born in Blois in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the fourth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-count-of-blois">Stephen-Henry</a>, Count of Blois and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a>, who was killed at the battle of Ramlah while on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> when Stephen was at most ten years old. His mother, Adela, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-flanders">Matilda of Flanders</a>, was known for her piety and strong personality and shaped his upbringing. Placed in the court of his uncle Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, he rose in prominence and was granted extensive lands. By his marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-i-countess-of-boulogne">Matilda of Boulogne</a> in 1125 he gained estates in Kent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>.</p><p>In 1120 Stephen planned to sail on the White Ship from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barfleur">Barfleur</a> but left it at the last moment. The ship sank with the heir to the throne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-adelin">William Adelin</a>, and nearly all its passengers, which left the succession open to challenge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Henry I died in 1135, Stephen crossed the Channel and took the throne with the help of his brother Henry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, arguing that keeping order took priority over his earlier oaths to support the Empress Matilda. In 1138 Robert, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a>, renounced his fealty and declared for Matilda, while Geoffrey of Anjou invaded Normandy and David I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> invaded northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Stephen created many new earldoms for loyal supporters, many of them for his advisor Waleran de Beaumont and his family.</p><p>Matilda and Robert landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 30 September 1139 with 140 knights. At the Battle of Lincoln on 2 February 1141 Stephen fought on foot, and after his sword broke he used a borrowed battle axe, but he was overwhelmed and taken prisoner. He was freed only after his wife and William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a> captured Robert at the Rout of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>. By 1147 England had suffered extensively, and the chronicle of the time recorded nothing but disturbance and wickedness and robbery. The royal coinage fragmented, and unauthorised castles multiplied.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Late in his reign Stephen sought to have his son Eustace crowned, but Pope Eugene III refused. In 1153 Henry, the Empress's son, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The armies met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallingford">Wallingford</a>, but neither side's barons wished to fight another pitched battle, and the sudden death of Eustace hastened peace. By the Treaty of Winchester Stephen recognised Henry as his heir, passing over his second son William. He died on 25 October 1154, and Henry II succeeded him. Modern historians have debated whether his personality, external events or weaknesses in the Norman state caused the long civil war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stephen, King of England” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The drowning of Henry’s only legitimate son, William Adelin, in the wreck of the White Ship off Barfleur in 1120 led, after Henry’s death in 1135, to a contested succession between <b>Stephen of Blois</b> and Henry’s daughter, the Empress Matilda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Rout of Winchester, where Robert of Gloucester, chief supporter of Empress Matilda, was captured, the two sides exchanged him for <b>King Stephen</b>, whom Matilda's forces had held.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-1">1 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The army of the imprisoned <b>King Stephen</b>, led by his queen, Matilda of Boulogne, blockaded Empress Matilda's forces at Winchester and crushed them as they retreated.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-14">14 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Countess Matilda married <b>Stephen of England</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-boulogne">County of Boulogne</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1138 <b>King Stephen</b> made Gilbert de Clare Earl of Pembroke, giving the lordship palatine powers.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-pembroke">County Palatine of Pembroke</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Blois, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Dover, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.59,1.33&amp;year=1132" aria-label="Blois on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4581 496 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Stephen, King of England"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5031 766h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5031 766h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.59,1.33&amp;year=1132">What was here in 1132? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
